      {
        "id": "char_1",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "CV",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "CVs show H adsorption/desorption peaks at Pt(110) (weakly bonded H, lower potential) and Pt(100) (strongly bonded H, higher potential) that shift positively by ~0.15 V from pH 0 to 13. HBE increases linearly with pH at -10 meV/pH for Pt(110) and -8 meV/pH for Pt(100).",
        "evidence_snippet": "The peak at lower potential in the H_UPD region corresponds to the H adsorption/desorption at the Pt(110) surface region (weakly bonded H), and the one at higher potential relates to the same process at Pt(100) (strongly bonded H). Both peaks shift positively by ∼0.15 V when the solution pH changes from 0 to 13."
      },
      {
        "id": "char_2",
        "type": "characterization",
        "method_name": "RDE",
        "is_reported": true,
        "characterization_summary": "RDE method shows HOR/HER activity decreases with increasing pH. Half-wave potential shifts positively by 0.2 V from pH 0.2 (1 M HClO4) to pH 12.8 (0.1 M KOH). Exchange current densities: 0.53 mA cm⁻²_Pt (0.1 M KOH, α=0.51), 0.63 mA cm⁻²_Pt (bicarbonate/carbonate buffer pH 10.7).",
        "evidence_snippet": "The starting point of the HOR plateau shifts positively by 0.2 V from the 1 M HClO4 solution (pH=0.2) to the 0.1 M KOH solution (pH=12.8), suggesting decreasing reaction rates with solution pH. The exchange current density for 0.1 M KOH is determined to be 0.53 mA cm⁻²_Pt (α=0.51).",
